Field Service Management System

System Design and Architecture Document

1. Project Requirements

Project Name
Field Service Management System

Target Users
- Customers who need home or business services
- Technicians who perform field jobs
- Managers who review requests and assign work
- Administrators who manage regions, categories, users, and approvals
- Super Admins who oversee the whole platform

Main Purpose
The Field Service Management System is designed to streamline the full service lifecycle from customer request submission to technician assignment, work execution, payment settlement, and post-service feedback. The system ensures transparency, proper scheduling, efficient technician allocation, secure payments, and better service quality tracking.

5–8 Core Features
1. Service Request Management
   - Customers can submit service requests with category, description, address, preferred date, and region.
2. Technician Assignment
   - Managers can review services and assign the most suitable technician based on skill, location, and availability.
3. Work Order Tracking
   - Each assigned service becomes a work order with status updates from assigned to in-progress to completed.
4. Technician Availability and Scheduling
   - Technicians can update availability and schedule their assigned jobs.
5. Service Reports and Documentation
   - Technicians submit job reports, notes, and supporting file attachments after service completion.
6. Payment Processing
   - Customers can pay for services using available payment integrations, including bKash.
7. Feedback and Ratings
   - Customers can review services and rate technician performance after completion.
8. Admin and Master Data Management
   - Admins manage regions, categories, skills, and user approvals.

User Roles
- SUPER_ADMIN: Full access to system configuration, records, and management controls.
- ADMIN: Manage categories, regions, user approvals, and platform operations.
- MANAGER: Reviews service requests, assigns technicians, and tracks work orders in a region.
- TECHNICIAN: Receives assigned jobs, updates reports, and completes field tasks.
- CUSTOMER: Submits service requests, tracks progress, pays, and provides reviews.

2. SDA Workflow

General Workflow
User → Frontend Request → API Route → Middleware/Authentication → Controller → Service / Business Logic → Prisma/Database → API Response → Frontend → User

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    U[Customer / Technician / Manager / Admin] --> F[Frontend Dashboard\nNext.js + UI]
    F --> A[API Request\nPOST /api/v1/service / login / assign / pay]

    subgraph Backend[API / Backend]
        R[Route Layer\nExpress Router]
        M[Middleware\nJWT Auth + Role Check + Validation]
        C[Controller\nHandles HTTP request]
        S[Service Layer\nBusiness logic and rules]
        D[Prisma / Database Access]
    end

    A --> R
    R --> M
    M --> C
    C --> S
    S --> V[Validate service request\nCheck category, date, region, permission]
    V --> B[Create / update business data\nService, Work Order, Payment, Feedback]
    B --> D
    D --> DB[(PostgreSQL\nPrisma ORM)]
    DB --> S
    S --> E[External Services\nCloudinary / bKash / Redis / Email]
    E --> S
    S --> RES[API Response\n{ success, statusCode, message, data }]
    RES --> F
    F --> U
```

Example Workflow: Submit Service Request
```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Customer logs in] --> B[Open service form in frontend]
    B --> C[POST /api/v1/service]
    C --> D[Route receives request]
    D --> E[Auth + Role Middleware\nverify JWT and CUSTOMER role]
    E --> F[Controller validates incoming request data]
    F --> G[Service Layer executes business logic]
    G --> H[Check category, region, date, and customer permission]
    H --> I[Create service record]
    I --> J[Auto-create linked work order]
    J --> K[Save to PostgreSQL via Prisma]
    K --> L[Return success response to frontend]
    L --> M[Dashboard refreshes and shows new service status]
    M --> N[Manager can view and assign technician]
```

Brief explanation of the important steps:
- User sends a request from the frontend when creating a service, login, payment, or feedback action.
- The request reaches the API route, which directs the traffic to the correct controller.
- Middleware checks authentication, user role, and validation rules before allowing the action.
- The controller receives the request and passes it to the service layer, where the main business logic is handled.
- The service layer validates the business rules, creates or updates records, and interacts with Prisma to save data in PostgreSQL.
- External services such as Cloudinary, bKash, Redis, or email are called when needed for file upload, payment processing, cache, or notifications.
- After processing, the backend returns a JSON response with success, status code, message, and data.
- The frontend receives the response, updates the state, and refreshes the relevant dashboard for the user.

This SDA workflow clearly follows the project structure: Client → API Request → Route → Middleware → Controller → Service → Database / External Services → API Response → Frontend.
3. Database ERD

Entity Relationship Diagram (dbdiagram.io style)
```dbml
Table users {
  id string [pk]
  name string
  email string [unique]
  password string
  profileImg string
  profileImgPublicId string
  googleId string [unique]
  facebookId string [unique]
  authProvider string
  emailVerified boolean
  role string
  status string
  needPasswordChange boolean
  isDeleted boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
}

Table customers {
  id string [pk]
  phone string
  address json
  isDeleted boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  userId string [unique, ref: > users.id]
}

Table technicians {
  id string [pk]
  phone string
  address json
  bio string
  nid string
  isProfileCompleted boolean
  isDeleted boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  status string
  rating float
  jobsCompleted int
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  userId string [unique, ref: > users.id]
}

Table managerProfiles {
  id string [pk]
  phone string
  address json
  nid string
  verificationStatus string
  rejectionReason string
  reviewdBy string
  reviewdAt datetime
  isDeleted boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  userId string [unique, ref: > users.id]
}

Table regions {
  id string [pk]
  area string [unique]
  description string
  isActive boolean
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
}

Table categories {
  id string [pk]
  name string [unique]
  icon string
  description string
  isActive boolean
  duration int
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
}

Table skills {
  id string [pk]
  name string [unique]
  icon string
  description string
  isActive boolean
  isDelete boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  categoryId string [ref: > categories.id]
}

Table services {
  id string [pk]
  title string
  description string
  priority string
  status string
  address json
  servicingDate date
  preferredStartTime time
  preferredEndTime time
  duration int
  isDeleted boolean
  isDeletedAt datetime
  assignedAt datetime
  reviewedBy string
  reviewedAt datetime
  rejectionReason string
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  customerId string [ref: > customers.id]
  categoryId string [ref: > categories.id]
  regionId string [ref: > regions.id]
}

Table workOrders {
  id string [pk]
  servicingDate datetime
  status string
  note string
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  regionId string [ref: > regions.id]
  customerId string [ref: > customers.id]
  technicianId string [ref: > technicians.id]
  serviceId string [unique, ref: > services.id]
  managerId string [ref: > managerProfiles.id]
}

Table availability {
  id string [pk]
  type string
  dayOfWeek int
  date date
  startTime time
  endTime time
  isActive boolean
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  technicianId string [ref: > technicians.id]
}

Table schedules {
  id string [pk]
  servicingDate datetime
  startTime datetime
  endTime datetime
  actualStart datetime
  actualEnd datetime
  status string
  isdeleted boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  technicianId string [ref: > technicians.id]
  workOrderId string [unique, ref: > workOrders.id]
}

Table serviceReports {
  id string [pk]
  reportUrl string
  reportPublicId string
  description string
  isDelete boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  workOrderId string [unique, ref: > workOrders.id]
}

Table payments {
  id string [pk]
  paymentId string [unique]
  amount decimal(10,2)
  method string
  transectionId string [unique]
  status string
  paidAt datetime
  payerReference string
  currency string
  merchantInvoiceNumber string [unique]
  getwayResponse json
  refundTrxId string
  refundAmount decimal(10,2)
  refundedAt string
  reason string
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  workOrderId string [unique, ref: > workOrders.id]
}

Table feedbacks {
  id string [pk]
  rating float
  comment string
  isDeleted boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  workOrderId string [unique, ref: > workOrders.id]
}

Table attachments {
  id string [pk]
  files json
  description string
  type string
  isDelete boolean
  deletedAt datetime
  createdAt datetime
  updatedAt datetime
  workOrderId string [ref: > workOrders.id]
}

Table technician_regions {
  technicianId string [ref: > technicians.id]
  regionId string [ref: > regions.id]
  indexes {
    (technicianId, regionId) [pk]
  }
}

Table manager_regions {
  managerId string [ref: > managerProfiles.id]
  regionId string [ref: > regions.id]
  indexes {
    (managerId, regionId) [pk]
  }
}
```

Important Relationships
- One User can have exactly one CustomerProfile, one TechnicianProfile, and one ManagerProfile.
- A CustomerProfile can create many Service records.
- A Region can be related to many services, many work orders, and many technician/manager profiles through the Prisma relation arrays.
- One Category can contain many Skills and many Services.
- One TechnicianProfile can have many Availability records, many Schedules, and many WorkOrder records.
- One Service is linked to exactly one WorkOrder, and the WorkOrder is created together with the service.
- A WorkOrder can have one ServiceReport, one Feedback, one Payment, and many Attachments.
- A WorkOrder belongs to one Region, one CustomerProfile, and optionally one TechnicianProfile and one ManagerProfile.
- Many technicians can have many skills via the implicit many-to-many relation defined on the TechnicianProfile.skills field.
- ManagerProfile and TechnicianProfile both maintain region relationships through many-to-many mappings.

4. Backend API Design

Base URL
/api/v1

Authentication and Authorization
- Public APIs: No authentication required.
- Protected APIs: Requires Authorization header with JWT access token.
- Roles: Some endpoints are restricted to ADMIN, MANAGER, TECHNICIAN, CUSTOMER, or SUPER_ADMIN.

API List

The backend exposes the following API endpoints based on the project’s route definitions.

